                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h3] Let's make this short [/h3]\n\nThe storytelling took a different direction from that of its predecessor, telling a story of its own. Here, the protagonists battled a criminal organisation called DIO, with Nakatomi as the main villain. However, the same tactic was employed: the main villain demanded absolute power in exchange for not destroying everything.\n\nThe presentation was highly entertaining, featuring cool, eye-catching battles throughout. You only had 72 in-game hours to complete your missions, but our heroes finish much faster than that!\n\nYou play as four different characters, each with their own weapons, abilities and power-ups. Little Lulu was the best area manager I have ever seen, while Leon and Toy loved charging head-on into everything. Although the playtime was just under an hour, there were two branching paths with a variety of cool missions. I did two playthroughs, but to really master the game you’d need a bit more, especially if you want all achievements.\n\n[h3] Controls [/h3]\n\nYou can dodge with jumps, shoot in a 360-degree arc with various weapons and power-ups, throw special grenades, detonate dynamite and charge up special attacks. You can also collect letters for bonus points and move sideways seamlessly and stylishly while holding down the fire button.\n\nYou also level up your power by slicing through enemies in melee combat, which granted you up to three weapon mastery ranks. Each level increased the variety of weapons and attack patterns, giving you an advantage in combat. As well as your primary weapon, you could collect various other weapons, such as a flamethrower, boomerang, rocket launcher or rechargeable 360 Blaster.\n\n[h3] Visuals [/h3]\n\nAside from the obvious, the game featured amazing details and impressive visual effects. While the main characters had their own distinct animations, the enemies weren't far behind. Each one had its own unique patterns, surprise attacks and arsenal. I was surprised at how varied they were; most would simply clean their mechs and jump right in when the protagonist arrived.\n\nThe art style was great, with quite a few variations in the levels and unique vibes. Unfortunately, the game suffered from significant frame drops, which were reduced to the tens in high-action combat. Whenever a battle started, the frame rate dropped significantly, which I didn't like at all.\n\n[h3] Level Design [/h3]\n\nThe level design was awesome, with cool layouts, hidden landscapes and mind-blowing world destruction. You started on the city streets and soon found yourself in the desert, where you encountered bombing jets, hidden military bases, train chases, HQ buildings, sea ports and even nuclear facilities. The atmosphere was fantastic, offering a great sense of progression and rewarding you with resistance fighters and flashy character appearances that enjoyed my brother and me for at least an hour in co-op.\n\n[h2] Final Verdict [/h2]\n\nAn amazing sequel to the original Shock Troopers. Although the performance and frame drops negatively impacted the overall gameplay experience, the presentation is excellent. It's perfect for an hour or two with a co-op partner if you want to experience both paths and mindlessly shoot up enemies.\n\n8/10",
